      },
    );

    test(
      'no platform pin: the host platform resolves the clipboard path',
      () async {
        // Exercises the host-[Platform] fallback arm of the platform
        // resolution (every other test pins `platform:` explicitly, so the
        // macOS/Linux/Windows detection never ran — CRAP 15.55 on the
        // merged-shard lcov, CI guards leg). The runner stays injected:
        // no real pasteboard is touched on any dev host.
        final runner = _RecordingRunner(0);

        final result = await writeClipboardText(
          'gho_TOKEN',
          runner: runner.call,
        );

        expect(result, isA<PasteboardWriteOk>());
        expect(runner.calls, hasLength(1));
        final expected = Platform.isMacOS
            ? 'pbcopy'
            : Platform.isWindows
            ? 'clip'
            : 'xclip';
        expect(runner.calls.single.$1, expected);
        // The credential rides stdin regardless of which arm ran.
        expect(runner.calls.single.$3, 'gho_TOKEN');
      },
    );
  });
}

The scoped lcov confirms exactly my analysis for `_effectivePlatform`:
- DA:54,1 — the signature line (function entry? line 54 is `String? _effectivePlatform(String? platform) {` — hit once)
- DA:56,1 — `if (platform != null) return platform;` — hmm wait, line 56 vs 57. Let me map: from the earlier read:
  - 54: `String? _effectivePlatform(String? platform) {`  — wait the read earlier showed:
    ```
    51: /// The effective platform id ...
    52-55: doc comment lines
    56: String? _effectivePlatform(String? platform) {
    57:   if (platform != null) return platform;
    58:   if (Platform.isMacOS) return 'macos';
    59:   if (Platform.isLinux) return 'linux';
    60:   if (Platform.isWindows) return 'windows';
    61:   return null;
    62: }
    ```
  Hmm but the CI census said `clipboard_writer.dart:54` for `_effectivePlatform` — that's the line crap4dart reports (54). And lcov DA lines: 54,1; 56,1; 57,1; 58,0; 65,1...

  So the actual line numbers in the CURRENT file: 54 = signature, 56 = `if (platform != null)...`, 57 = `if (Platform.isMacOS)...`, 58 = `if (Platform.isLinux)...`? That can't be — on Linux with the unpinned test, isLinux line MUST be hit. DA:58,0 means line 58 has 0 hits. Hmm, wait — but crap4dart reported 75% and CRAP 5.39 from THIS lcov. Lines for the function: 54, 56, 57, 58 → 3 of 4 hit = 75%. ✓

  So in the current file the function's executable lines are 54 (entry), 56 (`if (platform != null)`), 57 (`isMacOS` + return), 58 (`isLinux` + return)?? But then where are `isWindows` (60) and `return null` (61)? They don't appear in lcov at all — the Dart compiler didn't emit them?? Odd... unless the line numbers shifted: my earlier `read` of the file showed:

  54: `String? _effectivePlatform(String? platform) {`? No — the read showed:
  ```
  51: /// The effective platform id (`macos`/`linux`/`windows`): the explicit
  52: /// [platform] pin wins (unit tests — the macOS/Windows paths are
  53: /// unreachable on a Linux CI runner), else the host [Platform]. `null` =
  54: /// no clipboard writer for this platform.
  55: String? _effectivePlatform(String? platform) {
  56:   if (platform != null) return platform;
  57:   if (Platform.isMacOS) return 'macos';
  58:   if (Platform.isLinux) return 'linux';
  59:   if (Platform.isWindows) return 'windows';
  60:   return null;
  61: }
  ```
  Hmm, actually I don't remember the exact numbers from the read; the census says line 54 and lcov says DA:54,1 / DA:56,1 / DA:57,1 / DA:58,0. Given 4 tracked lines: 54 (entry), 56 (first if), 57 (second if), 58 (third if)? Then `return null` (59) untracked (merges with the if-chain region), `isWindows` missing?? 

  Wait — maybe the file I read earlier was slightly different from what I remember. Let me not guess; the counts speak: 4 lines tracked (54, 56, 57, 58), 3 hit (54, 56, 57), 1 unhit (58) → 75%. The unhit line 58 must be `if (Platform.isLinux) return 'linux';`... NO WAIT. On the Linux runner with the unpinned call, isLinux RETURNS — the line IS executed. Unless line 57 = `isLinux` (hit) and line 58 = `isWindows` (never hit on Linux). And line 56 = `if (platform != null)`. That maps:
  - 54: entry (hit)
  - 56: `if (platform != null) return platform;` (hit — false branch)
  - 57: `if (Platform.isMacOS) ...; if (Platform.isLinux) ...;` — hmm, two statements one line? No...

  Whatever the exact mapping — the measurement is what matters: 75% line coverage on this runner = the linux arm of the host detection executed (that's the only new execution my test adds), CRAP 5.39 OK. On CI (also Linux) the same. The `isWindows`/`return null` arms remain unhit — unreachable on Linux runners by design (that's exactly why the `platform:` pin seam exists), and the margin is huge (5.39 vs 12.0).

OK — now wait for the full suite re-run to finish and identify the 1 failed test.

